WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:06.270
On February 28, 1953, two scientists at the
University of Cambridge's Cavendish Laboratory

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.740
found the secret of life.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:13.040
Francis Crick and his young American assistant,
James Watson, discovered the structure of

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.040
DNA.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.359
And for that, they would go on to win the
Nobel Prize.

00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:19.609
But they couldn’t have done it without help.

00:00:19.609 --> 00:00:25.010
That help came from Rosalind Franklin, a 32-year-old
physical chemist who was little known outside

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:26.160
of her own scientific circles.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.710
But, here’s the thing.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:32.110
It was her research that pointed Crick and
Watson in the right direction.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:36.400
Watson had glimpsed one of Rosalind Franklin’s
X-ray photographs of DNA, which provided the

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.310
pivotal clue that helped determine DNA’s
double helix structure, the twisted ladder

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.850
that gave rise to modern molecular biology.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:49.790
Based on her notebooks, it’s clear that
Franklin was on the cusp of making the discovery

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:51.219
herself.

00:00:51.219 --> 00:00:56.930
Despite the critical role she did play, she
received no recognition for her work.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:01.500
Franklin was determined to become a scientist
at the age of twelve and overcame all obstacles

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:02.809
to get there.

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:05.549
England at that time did not exactly favor
women.

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:09.930
She was one of just 500 women accepted into
the University of Cambridge - the quota set

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:15.290
for female admissions to ensure they didn’t
exceed 10% of the male undergraduate body.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:19.799
She pursued her studies in physical chemistry
amid the terror of the London blitz in World

00:01:19.799 --> 00:01:21.259
War Two.

00:01:21.259 --> 00:01:25.560
Early in her career, she helped the war effort
by investigating how coal changed when subjected

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.530
to heat using a method called X-ray crystallography.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:34.250
When X-rays are shone onto a crystal, they
"bounce off" or "diffract" in specific patterns.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:38.979
By studying these patterns, scientists can
determine the structure of the crystal.

00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:43.630
She used the same technique to examine DNA,
which isn't a typical crystal, but she managed

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:44.820
to treat it like one.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.780
With the help of a young doctoral student,
Raymond Gosling, they shone X-rays on a tiny

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:55.240
DNA strand - the thickness of a strand of
hair - for 100 hours at a super close range

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:59.289
- 15 mm - in order to get a clear picture.

00:01:59.289 --> 00:02:05.060
Franklin's X-ray images of DNA, especially
the iconic Photograph 51, displayed a distinctive

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:06.870
X-shaped pattern.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:11.819
This pattern hinted at DNA’s real shape,
which resembled a spiral staircase or twisted

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:13.120
ladder.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.569
Interpreting the image required Franklin to
perform mathematical computations to analyze

00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:20.680
the pattern in an attempt to reveal its structure.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:25.150
While Franklin was busy doing just that at
King’s College London, where she worked,

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.450
Crick and Watson gained access to her X-ray
image.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:29.450
How?

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:33.680
Franklin’s assistant Gosling showed the
work to the assistant head of the biophysics

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.980
unit at King’s College London, Maurice Wilkins.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.000
Wilkins was supposed to be Franklin’s supervisor.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.959
But the two didn’t really get along.

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:46.290
Then, in January 1953, James Watson visited
King’s College London.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:51.590
And Wilkins showed it to his friend and rival
without Franklin’s knowledge or permission.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:56.659
Wilkins didn't realize the impact the photo
would have on Watson who later recalled: ‘The

00:02:56.659 --> 00:03:02.780
instant I saw the picture my mouth fell open
and my pulse began to race.’

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:07.530
During dinner, Wilkins went a step further
and gave Watson the necessary dimensions of

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:13.470
the DNA sample in Photo 51, which was then
used to develop the structure of DNA.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.080
And then there was more….

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:20.200
Watson and Crick later received an internal
King’s College London research report containing

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.930
Franklin’s unpublished results about her
DNA diffraction images.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:28.730
It was given to them by Max Perutz, a Cavendish
colleague, who got his hands on it during

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:30.640
a visit to King’s College.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.590
As Brenda Maddox writes in her biography about
Rosalind Franklin: “The MRC report was all

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:40.730
Watson and Crick could hope for — as valuable
as an enemy’s code book.”

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:45.760
From that report, they determined that the
two strands of the DNA helix must run in opposite

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.580
directions, like two escalators side-by-side,
one going up and one going down.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.140
Franklin hadn’t yet made this connection.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:56.430
But she was very close to figuring it out.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.720
Her notebook reveals she was contemplating
whether DNA had a double-helical, 2-chain

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.290
structure around the time Watson and Crick
got their hands on her report.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:10.650
Max Perutz defended handing over the report
to his Cambridge colleagues, “I was inexperienced

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:15.650
and casual in administrative matters and,
since the report was not confidential, I saw

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:17.820
no reason for withholding it.”

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:21.360
He later admitted he should have asked for
permission from Franklin “as a matter of

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.360
courtesy.”

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:27.120
As the report wasn’t confidential, it is
possible Franklin knew that her research had

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.199
already made its way to Cambridge.

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:34.580
Regardless, the Cambridge scientists did not
inform Franklin that they were using her data.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:39.150
Given all that she knew, how could Rosalind
Franklin have missed connecting the dots herself?

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.949
It boiled down to her personality.

00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:44.820
She was the kind of scientist who believed
in thoroughness.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.919
She didn't want to jump to conclusions based
on one image.

00:04:47.919 --> 00:04:52.600
DNA exists in different structures, notably
the A-form and B-form.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:57.750
While Photograph 51 showcased the B-form,
Franklin was captivated by the intricate details

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:03.210
of the A-form, believing it held more significant
and yet-unraveled insights, especially given

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.610
the richer data it presented to crystallographers.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:10.539
So, she temporarily set aside the now-iconic
Photo 51

00:05:10.539 --> 00:05:14.160
It's something like being handed the key to
a treasure chest but choosing to examine it

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.500
closely to make sure it would work before
using it.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.539
Watson and Crick didn’t make that mistake.

00:05:19.539 --> 00:05:24.000
They knew what they had and quickly brought
it to the world’s attention.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:30.930
On April 25, 1953, James Watson and Francis
Crick published a landmark paper in Nature,

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:35.710
proposing the double helix as the long elusive
structure of DNA, a discovery that earned

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:40.039
them the Nobel Prize in physiology or medicine
a decade later.

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:44.009
In the final paragraph of their paper, they
acknowledged that they had been “stimulated

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:48.810
by a knowledge of the general nature of the
unpublished experimental results and ideas”

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:54.280
of two scientists at King’s College London,
Wilkins and Franklin.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:00.490
In 1958, Franklin died of ovarian cancer,
possibly caused by her repeated exposure to

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.169
X-rays during her lifetime.

00:06:03.169 --> 00:06:05.880
She was just 37 years old.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.720
Franklin’s early death also meant she missed
out on the Nobel Prize - which is not given

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:12.230
posthumously.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:17.240
Neither Watson nor Crick mentioned her when
they accepted their awards in 1962, though

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.710
Wilkins, who also received the Nobel prize,
did.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:24.849
Historians say there’s no evidence Franklin
felt aggrieved by what happened.

00:06:24.849 --> 00:06:29.940
However, she may have felt differently had
she lived long enough to read an appallingly

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:35.870
disrespectful book written by Watson and published
in 1968 in which he admitted he and Crick

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:41.030
used Franklin’s data without her knowledge,
writing: “Rosy, of course, did not directly

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:42.120
give us her data.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.400
For that matter, no one at King’s realized
they were in our hands.”

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:49.860
Notice how he wrote disparagingly of Franklin,
calling her Rosy?

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:53.940
And he wasn’t alone in his casual diminishment
of her.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:59.080
When Franklin prepared to leave King’s College
for Birkbeck, part of the University of London,

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:04.729
Wilkins wrote to Crick: ‘Our dark lady is
leaving us next week.’

00:07:04.729 --> 00:07:09.129
Over the years, there has been a growing recognition
of the “dark lady’s” contributions to

00:07:09.129 --> 00:07:11.670
the discovery of the structure of DNA.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:16.129
With more people championing her role in one
of the most significant scientific discoveries

00:07:16.129 --> 00:07:18.320
of the 20th century.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:22.520
Finally giving her the credit that she didn’t
get when she was still alive.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:26.530
Franklin’s work was heavily rooted in scientific
thinking.

